2026-09-03T11:29Z UPSTREAM #48 CLOSED 2026-09-03, and it touches this rung only indirectly. The four things the maintainer built are backoff on 429, resume-from-missing-vector, the batch dials in the docs, and rate math plus passagesWithoutVectors in status. None of them is an uninstall surface and none is a durable pause, so gaps B and C stand as written. Gap A stands too: nothing in that release changes ZOTEUS_UPDATE_CHECK's default. What does change is the evidence about how to get things built here, recorded because this tracker's Actions ask for a filing form: a third party reported a field failure, this repository supplied the scoping and the seam by file and line at v1.12.0, and all four asks landed in one pass. Contained changes go as pull requests -- #27 and #28 merged verbatim -- and design-sized ones as issues with the seam named and a measured case attached. Gap A is the first kind.
2026-09-03T12:14Z GAP B'S VERB FRAMING IS RETIRED, per the author's ruling of 2026-09-03 (DECISIONS.md): a documented removal procedure IS an uninstall surface, and the harness may execute it verbatim and sweep. The earlier reading -- that zoteus must grow a callable uninstall verb before this cell can be green -- rested on R15's body sentence alone and did not survive the layer under it. The ratified interface's not-offered state exists for a target with no uninstall surface, and an external MCP server has no host uninstall lifecycle to hook, so under the old reading goal 1 was unkeepable BY CONSTRUCTION for that whole architecture class, for a reason no user would notice. That is an instrument requirement leaking into a product one. The body above is restated rather than annotated, because leaving both readings in a tracker is how a tracker stops being read. What the gap now asks for is a published removal procedure, which is a contained documentation change of the form that has merged upstream verbatim twice, rather than a design-sized issue the maintainer builds. Two conditions the ruling attaches, and they bind this ticket: the arena must redirect TMPDIR and the XDG roots before the clause can be graded at all -- measured today, only HOME is redirected and only in one adapter -- and the documented-steps path must be seen red against stub-uninstall-leaves-residue and stub-under-declares before any green is trusted.
2026-09-03T12:38Z claude note THE BASELINE MOVED UNDER THIS TRACKER'S CELLS, which were taken against b05ed69 (v1.12.0). UPSTREAM now names v1.13.0 / b0e0bc8 (ticket 0618), so every cell in the matrix is now measured against a superseded revision and must be re-derived before it is argued. What the re-read establishes for this rung specifically: gap A stands unchanged -- ZOTEUS_UPDATE_CHECK still defaults true and src/lib/update-check.ts is untouched by the whole range, so the R10-no-egress fail on the release-endpoint name lookup reproduces by construction. Gap B stands: no uninstall surface arrived. Gap C stands and is now re-verified in source rather than inherited: zotero_index's action enum is unchanged at build|refresh|update|status|stop, so there is still no pause and README's R22 row keeps its 'verified absent'. One thing did move that this rung's R15 term will care about: mcpb/manifest.json drops the allow_electron_fulltext user_config field and adds embedding_dtype, so an existing desktop install silently loses a setting it had ticked -- unknown variables are ignored by design, which is a declaration question rather than a residue one. Nothing here is measured; this entry records that the pin moved.
2026-09-03T12:48Z SAFETY CORRECTION TO GAP B, from the author on reading the ruling: the harness transcribes PATHS and executes nothing. The ruling's containment condition -- every path a step touches must resolve inside the arena -- was a code check standing between an externally-authored command line and his home directory, with ordinary failure modes: a symlink inside the arena pointing out, HOME expanded late, a relative path after a cd, a glob. R15 grades derived state, so the only thing this clause ever needs from a published procedure is the list of locations it tells the user to delete; the adapter transcribes those paths, the harness removes exactly them itself without a shell, and a documented step that is not a path is recorded rather than performed since an installed package is not target-created derived state. Two preconditions this adds to the ones already on the ticket: the episode runs inside sandbox.py's pluggable namespace with only the declared roots bound read-write, and the arena should belong to a dedicated account rather than sitting under the operator's home with only HOME redirected, which is the weakest point in the current setup.
2026-09-03T12:52Z THE DEDICATED ACCOUNT IS MANDATORY, ruled by the author 2026-09-03: 'The alt user is not optional.' The amendment logged above had it as a preference -- where an account is available it should own the arena -- and that reading is withdrawn. It is a precondition of this rung's R15 cell being gradeable at all, alongside the TMPDIR and XDG redirection already recorded, and it binds every assertion rather than only the uninstall clause: the harness installs software it does not control and sweeps a filesystem afterwards, and it has been doing both as the operator. sandbox.py's namespace does not substitute -- it is bound to the R10 egress arm, the residue and durability assertions run outside it, and it is unusable rootless on the second machine. Ticket 0625 carries the implementation.
2026-09-03T13:43Z FORM DECIDED, AND DEFERRED: the author ruled we will NOT open an issue asking upstream to write an uninstall doc -- we write UNINSTALL.md ourselves, in the project's own documentation style (docs/configuration.md and docs/semantic-search.md's voice and structure), and submit it as a docs pull request, the contained form that has merged verbatim twice (#27, #28). Explicitly not this session. What the draft needs when it is picked up: the declared derived-state roots from bench/acceptance/adapters/zoteus.py's Declaration (data dir, model cache under it since PR #27), phrased as removal steps a user follows, and the historical case as its worked example -- before #27 'delete the data directory' would have been wrong, because the model cache lived outside it. This is what the carve-out ruling's residue sweep will grade once the doc exists, so the draft and the carve-out's TMPDIR/XDG-redirection and dedicated-account preconditions can land in either order.
2026-09-03T18:15Z note Gap B (0630) delivered: UNINSTALL.md drafted at verification/UNINSTALL-DRAFT-0630.md, staged not sent, hand-off left to the GOVERNANCE.md contained-PR lane. TMPDIR/XDG precondition RE-MEASURED and still open, sharper than this log's earlier reading: zoteus.py's _env() sets only ZOTEUS_* vars and merges them onto the full operator os.environ (line 372), redirecting nothing else -- not even HOME, which beaver.py, zotero_core_6012.py, zotero_mcp.py and zotseek.py all do redirect. TMPDIR is redirected nowhere under bench/acceptance/; XDG_ appears once, XDG_CACHE_HOME in zotero_mcp.py only; sandbox.py has no TMPDIR/XDG/HOME handling. R15 cannot be graded green for zoteus until that closes; the doc's correctness does not depend on it. The dedicated-account precondition is closed (0625). Tracker stays open: gap C (R22) untouched.
2026-09-03T18:18Z claude note GAP A ATTRIBUTED (ticket 0629), and it needs no second cause: all four DNS lookups on the R10-no-egress subject arm are ONE name lookup. Measured on this machine through the acceptance layer's own tracer and sandbox (bench/probe_getaddrinfo_shape.py -> bench/results/0629-gap-a/syscall-shape.json): one getaddrinfo of a non-resolving name costs exactly 4 connect(127.0.0.53:53) under --unshare-net and 3 with a route, against a null arm at 0/0 and a numeric-address connect at off_machine 1, dns 0 -- so the four are the resolver's per-lookup signature, not four application-level lookups. Both numbers match acceptance-zoteus-v1130.json on both arms, and that artifact's own _EGRESS_PROBE control (one getaddrinfo) read dns 4 isolated and dns 3 shared in the same run. Source side, read from a real fork/ built at the reviewed SHA b0e0bc8: exactly one network-shaped call is reachable from startup in the acceptance configuration -- src/lib/update-check.ts:93, one unauthenticated GET to api.github.com, wired at src/server.ts:184-190 and gated on ZOTEUS_UPDATE_CHECK (src/config.ts:237 declares bool(true), :408 carries it). The cloud-key probe at src/router/capabilities.ts:40 is guarded by web.hasKey and the run logged cloud=none; the desktop probe at :55-56 targets an IP literal and costs no lookup; the local transformers runtime left no residual to explain. So Action 3's upstream form has ONE cause to name, not several: the default-true update check, one fetch, one lookup. The confirming ZOTEUS_UPDATE_CHECK=false arm did NOT run -- every target-process assertion reports not-run on this machine because /etc/sudoers.d/acceptance-tester is absent (0625's posture landed at 16:15 today, after the v1130 artifact was taken at 15:44); the one-line remedy and the exact re-run command are in bench/results/0629-gap-a/README.md.
2026-09-04T05:32Z GAP C RE-DERIVED AGAINST THE REVIEWED BASELINE (b0e0bc8/v1.13.0, fork/ rebuilt via make upstream-checkout + npm ci + npm run build). Confirms the adapter docstring's three claims by source, not by its own argument, and finds a fourth thing the ticket did not have. (a) stop's cancel flag really is in-memory only: requestStop() (index-manager.ts:742-746) sets this.cancelToken.cancelled=true on a plain object, nothing else. (b) the checkpoint really reaches disk, unconditionally, on cancellation: buildIncremental's exit (index-manager.ts:1370-1381) calls noteCheckpoint() then awaits persistNow() on both the cancelled and completed path; persist() is this.save() (line 1012), which for sqlite 'commits its open transaction here' (line 1010-1011); the checkpoint lives in the same file, meta table, key 'checkpoint' (sqlite-index.ts:928, :1092). (c) build really is the only function that continues an interrupted build, and it really cannot be separated from the destructive branches: resumeFrom() (index-manager.ts:871-880) returns the checkpoint unless opts.fresh, and fresh is set only by action:'refresh' (build.ts:123) -- but action:'update' ALSO resumes when it must: updateBlocker() (index-manager.ts:1409-1422) refuses a delta when a checkpoint exists with no stamp and says outright the full build 'RESUMES that one rather than starting over'; startIndexUpdate (build.ts:472-480) answers that refusal by calling startIndexBuild, the identical function action:'build' calls -- no third function exists. repairSearchIndex (repair.ts) runs inside that same RPC, before buildIncremental, whenever the store is faulted (index-tool.ts:96-106) -- true of action:'refresh' too, since the fault-check only special-cases 'update' (index-tool.ts:82-95), which repair.ts's own comment ('Only ever called for an explicit action:build', repair.ts:15) does not quite say -- a minor doc/code gap, not load-bearing since refresh already discards on purpose. So a target with resume mapped onto build could not be resume-only even in principle: the same RPC silently repairs when the store is unreadable, state the caller does not control ex ante. Confirmed, not merely argued.
2026-09-04T05:32Z FOURTH FINDING: ZOTEUS_INDEX_AUTO_REFRESH -- the variable this ticket's 2026-09-03T12:38Z entry and Gap C's own body sentence name as the reason the restart clause's falsifying arm is disabled -- does not exist at the reviewed baseline. Grepped case-insensitively across the whole built src/, dist/, docs/ tree (zero hits, checked separately from config.ts); config.ts's full ~50-key ZOTEUS_ zod schema (lines 182-259) has no such key. config.ts's own comment on the adjacent, actually-removed ZOTEUS_ALLOW_ELECTRON_FULLTEXT says why this is silent rather than a startup error: 'unknown variables are ignored' (line above 218). Separately: no code path in src/ calls startIndexBuild or startIndexUpdate except index-tool.ts's explicit action handler and semantic-search.ts's auto_build fallback, which the adapter's own query() already disables (auto_build:False, zoteus.py:415) -- server.ts has zero references to either starter. So on this baseline nothing auto-resumes on restart, unconditionally, whether or not ZOTEUS_INDEX_AUTO_REFRESH is set, and setting it false (every harness script under bench/ has, since before this ticket) changes nothing the restart arm would measure. This removes one of the two things Gap C cited as reasons the restart clause is untested -- not by fixing it, but by showing it was never a live risk at this baseline. Gap C's body paragraph is restated below to drop it (source-verified reading, not a ruling; this log preserves the prior text rather than silently correcting it).
2026-09-04T05:32Z THE SURVIVING BLOCKER IS DOUBLE, not single as this ticket previously said. check_pause_stops_background_work and check_pause_holds_across_restart (assertions.py:779, :853) each gate on two things. The first alone already stops zoteus: _the_change_creates_work (assertions.py:639) checks durability.work_counters(control) is None (assertions.py:717; durability.py work_counters(), line 112) BEFORE the graded target's pause is ever called, and zoteus's status() never populates a work/counters object (zoteus.py _work_counters(), None on every run measured) -- matches this ticket's prior wording. But a target that shipped real work.<stage>.<trigger>.<outcome> counters tomorrow would hit a SECOND, currently-invisible wall: both clauses' only perturbation is hardcoded durability.EDIT_ONE_ITEM (assertions.py:733, :833, :927) -- edit one item in the library -- and zoteus's own perturb() (zoteus.py:475-480) already refuses it, on a principled R15 ground (a write to the user's own Zotero library, excluded from derived state) that 0033's ledger work does not touch and would not remove. So 0033 is necessary but not sufficient for gap C: shipping SPEC.md par5.2.8-shaped counters closes the first gate and immediately opens the second, on a target that has never been able to run EDIT_ONE_ITEM and by design never will. This is appended to 0033's own log as a note, not argued there, per the append-only rule.
2026-09-04T05:32Z CHEAP OBSERVABLE, concretely, and why the counters route does not fit it. zoteus's status already reports genuine (not fabricated) documents/vectors/passagesWithoutVectors (index-manager.ts:722-725, documents-vectors when an embedder is configured and vectors are not stale) as flat top-level fields, not nested under work/counters, so durability.work_counters()'s search (zoteus.py WORK_OBJECT_KEYS, work/counters keys only) correctly does not pick them up -- they carry no trigger classification (new/edit/resync/... per SPEC.md 5.2.8), so packaging them as work.<stage>.<trigger>.<outcome> rows would be the dishonest-counter trap durability.py's perturb() docstring itself warns against ('a harness that reached past the adapter... would be grading a state it had manufactured', durability.py perturb(), line 192). But R22's own arithmetic (_pause_verdict, assertions.py:611-636) only asks whether ANY done-outcome counter moved, no trigger needed. And the sqlite file these counts live in is already opened directly by this adapter, read-only, for the schema-version check (zoteus.py _index()/_stamp(), lines 337-357, 483-490) -- the same technique reads SELECT COUNT(*) FROM passages and SELECT COUNT(*) FROM passages WHERE vector IS NOT NULL (schema: sqlite-index.ts:930-938) and the meta row keyed 'checkpoint' (sqlite-index.ts:1092), bypassing status() entirely. Proposed, not implemented: an R22-only check, parallel to the existing two, that (1) drives a NEW perturbation -- resume embedding against a seeded index with passagesWithoutVectors greater than 0, via action:'build' -- through zoteus's existing perturb() hook rather than around it, since EDIT_ONE_ITEM is refused on principle and always will be; (2) grades on the vector count and checkpoint read directly from the index file rather than on work.<stage>.<trigger>.<outcome>, since the only claim at stake is whether embedding continued, not whether it was proportional to a named trigger. Design-sized (a new perturbation kind plus a standalone check, review-round history like 0033's own PR #255 suggests several rounds), not a same-session prototype. Filed as tickets/0636, deliberately not Blocked-by 0033/0032: the finding above is that their counters alone would not unblock R22. Action 5 (of this ticket's own numbering) done: 0033's log carries the double-blocker note.
