2026-09-04T10:17Z codex note CORRECTION TO 08:37Z AND 08:50Z: artifact 0643 does not decide R22. It proves only that the staged counters and build-triggered perturbation execute; its FAIL comes from explicit action:build after pause while SPEC.md says explicit build while paused asks, and the restart arm excludes automatic startup work. R22 is UNDECIDED pending master 0665's faithful repair and remeasurement.
--- body ---
## Context

Goal 1 of the ladder (`README.md`): *nothing leaves this machine unasked, one
obvious switch stops the work, and uninstall removes every declared piece of
derived state and leaves no undeclared residue.* Three terms since 2026-09-03,
when R31 was retired: **R10, R15, R22**. It is the lowest rung because its
assertions need no corpus, no build and no library.

This tracker holds one question: **is goal 1 kept for zoteus?** Today it is not,
and the reasons are below. The assertions all exist — seven checks in
`bench/acceptance/assertions.py`, each with a fail-control — so what is missing
is the target's side, not the instrument's.

## Where the rung stands for zoteus

From `bench/results/0604-ladder-matrix/MATRIX.md`, run 2026-09-03 against
revision 1.12.0:

| clause | state | why |
|---|---|---|
| `R10-local-by-default` | **pass** | local embedder, active |
| `R10-no-egress` | **FAIL** | gap A below |
| `R15-residue-inventory` | **pass** | 0 residue outside 1 declared root |
| `R15-model-cache-under-declared-roots` | **pass** | upstream PR #27's doing |
| `R15-uninstall-removes-declared-state` | not-offered | gap B below |
| `R22-pause-stops-background-work` | not-run | gap C below |
| `R22-pause-holds-across-restart` | not-run | gap C below |

Three of seven green. The rung is a conjunction, so that is not partial credit.

## The gaps, each with the sentence that establishes it

**Gap A — the default configuration phones home (R10). Closed out; sent
upstream as oscardvs/zoteus#54.** *Restated 2026-09-04 after the confirming
arm actually ran (ticket 0637 fixed the harness bug that blocked it) — the
prior reading is preserved in the log rather than silently corrected.*
`ZOTEUS_UPDATE_CHECK` defaults to true in the reviewed build, and the server
logged "Update available" during the run. R10: *"Without an explicit opt-in,
my library text and my queries MUST NOT leave this machine"*, with the
one-time model-weight download named as the sole exception. **Now directly
measured, not only argued:** the confirming arm (`ZOTEUS_UPDATE_CHECK=false`)
ran live and read `dns 0`, against the default configuration's `dns 4` in the
same session — one `fetch()` in `update-check.ts` fully explains the failure,
with no second cause. The graded `R10-no-egress` verdict for the default
configuration is unchanged (still **FAIL**) since nothing in zoteus itself
moved; only an upstream fix or a harness re-measure after one would move it.

**Gap B — nothing published says how to remove it (R15).** *Restated
2026-09-03 after a ruling; the earlier reading, that zoteus must grow a callable
uninstall verb, is retired — see the log.* R15's body requires that "the target's
real uninstall surface removes them" and that a residue inventory check the
declaration is complete. For an external MCP server with no host uninstall
lifecycle to hook, that surface is a **published removal procedure**, and the
ruling of 2026-09-03 lets the harness execute one verbatim and sweep. So what is
missing is not a verb: it is documentation, and its absence is what makes the
clause ungradeable.

Upstream PR #27 (merged 2026-08-29) is what makes a correct procedure *writable*
at all, by pinning the model cache under the data directory — before it, "delete
the data directory" would have been wrong, which is exactly the failure the sweep
exists to catch. §5.2.7's maintenance purge is not a candidate for this role and
never was: it is checkpoint plus VACUUM plus compaction, which keeps a living
index rather than removing one.

**Gap C — R22 is undecided pending the acceptance repair in master ticket
0665.** *Restated 2026-09-04 after the global R22 audit; the earlier readings
and the 0643 diagnostic remain in the append-only log.* Ticket 0643 proved that
the staged work counters and build-triggered perturbation execute end to end,
but its two FAILs are not faithful R22 verdicts. The checks pause only after
work settles, discard work that resumes during restart before taking their
baseline, and the zoteus fallback explicitly calls `action:build` after pause
although `SPEC.md §5.2.7` says an explicit build while paused asks. The harness
observes no ask or consent boundary. Master 0665 owns active-work stopping,
startup-inclusive restart measurement, all work classes, resume semantics,
required-live deletion/query paths and an honest real-target re-verdict. Until
that closes, this tracker records R22 as undecided; 0643 is retained only as
evidence that the counter and perturbation mechanisms can run.

## Actions

1. Settle gap C's measurement through master 0665. The 0643 diagnostic is not a
   current R22 verdict; remeasure only after the acceptance semantics are
   faithful to the specification.
2. Run gap A's discriminating arm and attribute the four lookups. Done
   2026-09-04: ran live once 0637 fixed the posture/namespace ordering,
   `dns 4` default vs `dns 0` disabled — see log.
3. Decide the upstream form of gaps A and B against `GOVERNANCE.md` and
   `SYNC.md`'s measured asymmetry, and file.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] all seven goal-1 clauses green for zoteus, each resting on a run
- [ ] no clause green by waiver, and none green where its falsifying arm was
      disabled in the run that passed it

## Why one tracker per rung, per target

The ladder's rungs are conjunctions: a rung is kept when every one of its members
holds, and at no state before that. Nothing in the repository asserted that
conjunction *for a named target* — `README.md` reports each requirement's
standing across the project, and `bench/results/0604-ladder-matrix/MATRIX.md`
reports each clause against each target, but neither answers "is this rung kept
for zoteus". These three trackers answer exactly that, one each, and they close
only when their rung's every clause is green for zoteus.

zoteus is the named target because *works for me* is the acceptance standard and
this is the tool the author runs. A green here is a green for zoteus and for
nothing else; the other four roster targets are measured by the same layer and
are not this tracker's business.

## Children

Children are filed when their work starts, not up front. The gaps below are
enumerated with the requirement sentence each rests on, which is what a child
ticket would carry anyway; filing four stubs today would inflate the queue
without advancing anything (severity floor, `rules/workflow.md`). This tracker is
edited to list each child as it is created.

| child | scope | state |
|---|---|---|
